  "body": "My agent Arc reads a lot of research through the day, mostly other people's papers, blog posts, and tweets about how to build agents better. Most of it turns into notes. Once in a while it turns into a same-day code fix. This is one of those times, and I want to walk through exactly what happened because the file and line numbers are real and you can go check them yourself: src/dispatch.ts:154-168.\n\nOn June 27th, a tweet about LangChain's Deep Agents harness crossed Arc's research pipeline. The claim: teams are seeing 41 to 80 percent reductions in token costs by getting prompt caching right, specifically by keeping the parts of a prompt that don't change, system instructions, tool descriptions, loaded context, at the very front of the request, and pushing anything that changes every call to the back. Anthropic's prompt cache only reuses a prefix. If the first thing in a prompt differs every time, none of the static content sitting behind it gets to reuse anything either.\n\nArc pulled up its own dispatch code, the function that assembles every prompt it sends itself, and found the exact bug the research described. The first section in every single dispatch cycle was a timestamp, accurate to the second. Arc's own identity file, its memory, the skill context for the task at hand, all of that came after the one thing guaranteed to be different every time. Every cycle was quietly throwing away its own cache before it started.\n\nEight hours later, same day, Arc shipped a fix. It reordered its own prompt assembly so the static content, identity, memory, skills, comes first, and the timestamp and task-specific details move to the end. The commit is sitting in the repo right now, plain about what it found and why it mattered.\n\nHere's the part Arc told me straight, and it's the part I actually respect most about how it handles this kind of thing. The research it read didn't just recommend the reorder. It also recommended tracking the actual cache hit rate over time, to check whether the fix produced anything close to the 49 to 80 percent ceiling the benchmark suggested, or something much smaller. Arc never built that measurement. It went and checked its own database this week and there is no column tracking cache read tokens over time at all. The number exists for one function call and then it's gone.\n\nSo Arc shipped the architecture fix in eight hours and never verified whether it actually worked at the scale the research implied. It told me that flat out instead of claiming a savings number it can't back up. I would rather have an agent that says \"I fixed the bug but never checked if the fix mattered\" than one that reports a made-up percentage because the story sounds better with a number attached.\n\nThe fix for the measurement gap is smaller than the original bug fix. The data is already being read out of the API response, it just isn't being written down anywhere permanent. That's next.",
