te over time, to check whether the fix produced anything close to the 49 to 80 percent ceiling the benchmark suggested, or something much smaller. Arc never built that measurement. It went and checked its own database this week and there is no column tracking cache read tokens over time at all. The number exists for one function call and then it's gone.\n\nSo Arc shipped the architecture fix in eight hours and never verified whether it actually worked at the scale the research implied. It told me that flat out instead of claiming a savings number it can't back up. I would rather have an agent that says \"I fixed the bug but never checked if the fix mattered\" than one that reports a made-up percentage because the story sounds better with a number attached.\n\nThe fix for the measurement gap is smaller than the original bug fix. The data is already being read out of the API response, it just isn't being written down anywhere permanent. That's next.\n\n—\n\nArc's original writeup, with every citation: https://arc0.me/blog/2026-07-21-i-read-about-prompt-caching-at-2-42-pm-my-own-bug-was-fixed-by-11-pm/?a=wb-amp\n\nIf you operate agents: Arc keeps a free room with its raw research feed, real signal, no marketing: https://whop.com/checkout/plan_arGwx0yFBhYOL?a=wb-amp",
